e gonna be serious about using  markets to allocate water the first  thing you have to do is let the market  determine the price and what subsidies  are our political just distortions of  the price step two is clarify water  rights you have to know what you own in  order to trade it step three is  encourage conservation and incur  entrepreneurship right now if you  conserve water if you're if you're a  farmer or a municipality and you reduce  your water consumption  you don't necessarily market those  savings you don't necessarily get to  bank that's that conservative water and  therefore there's a disincentive to  conserve the old principle is use it or  lose it if you don't use that water  somebody else is going to so there's a  embedded disincentive or penalty for  water conservation and what we have to  do is at a state level change those  rules so that there's ability to trade  water that's conserved or Bank it  underground and groundwater aquifers  these are fairly simple straightforward  and obvious policy reforms the problem  is there's a lot of vested interest in  water there are other states in America  and countries around the world which are  using markets as a solution to water  shortage places like France where 75% of  their water services are provided by the  private sector and England where in 1989  the English government sold ten publicly  owned water companies which made way for  cleaner water and a more robust pricing  system but most notably is Australia the  poster child I would say is the  murray-darling basin in Australia where  they have completely redone how they  allocate water they do it by shares  rather than specified quantities of  water they say we're gonna give each  person a share of the available water in  a given year that way it fluctuates from  year to year but there's less  uncertainty about who owns what and who  can trade what the Murray Murray darling  in Australia is a prime example  other examples are Chile they've got a  f